# vi_pll.cfg -- optional. Put this (renamed to vi_pll.cfg) in the ROOT of the USB
# drive (or SD). If present, it REPLACES the built-in control+field-map sweep with
# the explicit tries listed here. Edit on your PC between runs -- no rebuild needed.
#
# Syntax (data only; malformed lines are skipped and noted in the log):
#   # comment or blank line            -> ignored
#   TRY    OFF=VAL [OFF2=VAL2 ...]      -> one experiment, anchored at 1280x240p/54MHz
#   TRYRAW OFF=VAL [OFF2=VAL2 ...]      -> one experiment, current mode kept (no anchor)
#
#   OFF = Hollywood register offset in hex. ONLY these offsets are accepted
#         (they are the ones the tool snapshots and restores):
#             1A0 1A4 1A8 1AC 1C4 1C8
#   VAL = full 32-bit value to write, in hex.
#
# Each experiment is logged write-ahead, measured, then the ORIGINAL values are
# restored. All of these registers also reset on a console power cycle.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECODED from run 1 (0x1C4, boot 0x002006C0 = 54 MHz):
#   byte1  bits 8-15  = FEEDBACK MULTIPLIER.  Fout ~ (54/7) * Nfb,  Nfb = byte1 + 1.
#                       Clean & linear 7..23 (54..177 MHz).  <-- main clock knob.
#   byte0  bits 0-7   = OUTPUT DIVIDER.  bit0 set (0xC1) => /2.  (full encoding TBD)
#   byte2  bits 16-23 = SHARED/CRITICAL CLOCK.  Writing 0x21 HARD-HANGS the console.
#                       ***NEVER set byte2 to anything but 0x20.***
#   bit31             = PLL-lock status, set by hardware; ignore.
#   0x1A0 bit31       = inert (status mirror), not a usable divider control.
# 0x1C8 (boot D870700E) = still being mapped; candidate FINE control for exact clocks.
#
# Predicted multiplier values (byte0=0xC0, byte2=0x20):
#   Nfb= 9 -> 1C4=002008C0 ~ 69.4 MHz     Nfb=10 -> 1C4=002009C0 ~ 77.1 MHz  (720p bracket)
#   Nfb=19 -> 1C4=002012C0 ~146.6 MHz     Nfb=20 -> 1C4=002013C0 ~154.3 MHz  (1080i bracket)
# Targets: 74250 kHz (720p/1080i) and 148500 kHz sit BETWEEN integer Nfb steps, so the
# fine tuning must come from byte0 (divider) and/or 0x1C8.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ROUND-3 EXAMPLES (uncomment/edit). Lines run top-to-bottom; a hang stops the rest,
# so put your most-wanted tries first. Keep 0x1C4 byte2 = 0x20 ALWAYS.
#
# Bracket 148.5 MHz and try fine-tuning it down with byte0 and 0x1C8:
# TRY 1C4=002013C0
# TRY 1C4=002012C0
# TRY 1C4=002012C0 1C8=D870700F
# TRY 1C4=002012E0
#
# Bracket 74.25 MHz:
# TRY 1C4=002009C0
# TRY 1C4=002008C0
# TRY 1C4=002008C0 1C8=D870700F
